1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,376
RentAxis separates access by organization,
role, and property assignment.

2
00:00:04,376 --> 00:00:09,724
This tutorial shows how those boundaries
affect the interface and why

3
00:00:09,724 --> 00:00:15,072
an unavailable menu is often correct
behavior rather than an error.

4
00:00:15,072 --> 00:00:18,476
Start with the prepared Super Admin
account.

5
00:00:18,476 --> 00:00:22,366
A super administrator can provision
organizations and move

6
00:00:22,366 --> 00:00:26,255
between tenant contexts for authorized
support and administration.

7
00:00:26,255 --> 00:00:30,145
Open the organization switcher and note
the currently

8
00:00:30,145 --> 00:00:34,035
active organization before selecting the
Tutorial Demo organization.

9
00:00:34,035 --> 00:00:37,438
The page reloads inside that tenant
context.

10
00:00:37,438 --> 00:00:40,355
Switching context does not copy, merge,

11
00:00:40,355 --> 00:00:43,273
or expose records from another
organization.

12
00:00:43,273 --> 00:00:46,190
Always verify the organization name before

13
00:00:46,190 --> 00:00:48,621
creating, editing, or deleting data.

14
00:00:48,621 --> 00:00:52,997
Next, sign in with the prepared Tenant
Admin account.

15
00:00:52,997 --> 00:00:57,373
This role manages users, portfolio data,
leasing, finance, settings,

16
00:00:57,373 --> 00:01:01,749
and enabled services for one organization,
but it cannot

17
00:01:01,749 --> 00:01:05,638
provision unrelated tenants or grant
itself platform-level authority.

18
00:01:05,638 --> 00:01:09,528
Compare the sidebar with the Super Admin
view.

19
00:01:09,528 --> 00:01:12,445
Now use the Property Manager account.

20
00:01:12,445 --> 00:01:16,821
Property managers operate only the
properties assigned to them.

21
00:01:16,821 --> 00:01:20,225
Open the property list and confirm that

22
00:01:20,225 --> 00:01:23,142
an unassigned demo property is absent.

23
00:01:23,142 --> 00:01:28,490
Directly navigating to an unassigned
record must not bypass that restriction.

24
00:01:28,490 --> 00:01:33,838
The Tenant User role is more limited and
should be used

25
00:01:33,838 --> 00:01:38,700
for staff who need a defined operational
surface without tenant-wide

26
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:39,187
administration.

27
00:01:39,187 --> 00:01:41,618
Continue with the Renter account.

28
00:01:41,618 --> 00:01:45,994
The renter sees their tenancy, payments,
maintenance, meetings, resident

29
00:01:45,994 --> 00:01:49,883
services, and marketplace features rather
than internal administration.

30
00:01:49,883 --> 00:01:52,801
Finally, review the Security Guard role.

31
00:01:52,801 --> 00:01:55,718
A guard works with assigned-property
visitor

32
00:01:55,718 --> 00:01:58,635
queues, scans, approvals, admissions, and
exits.

33
00:01:58,635 --> 00:02:03,983
They do not receive access to leases,
finance, or tenant configuration.

34
00:02:03,983 --> 00:02:06,414
Feature access also affects navigation.

35
00:02:06,414 --> 00:02:10,790
Listings, Meetings, Email Notifications,
Lease Renewals, and Gate Pass

36
00:02:10,790 --> 00:02:15,166
can be enabled per organization, so a role
may

37
00:02:15,166 --> 00:02:19,542
be valid while its related feature remains
intentionally hidden.

38
00:02:19,542 --> 00:02:23,432
Finish by returning to the Super Admin
account

39
00:02:23,432 --> 00:02:27,321
and switching back to the original
organization context.

40
00:02:27,321 --> 00:02:32,670
The safe operating habit is simple: check
the tenant, check the

41
00:02:32,670 --> 00:02:37,532
role, and check the property assignment
before every sensitive action.

42
00:02:37,532 --> 00:02:42,394
If access appears wrong, do not borrow a
broader account.

43
00:02:42,394 --> 00:02:47,256
Ask an authorized administrator to correct
the role or assignment.
